// Recursive JavaScript function to traverse a DOM tree and find elements matching a given selector
function FindElementsBySelector(element, selector) {
    // Check if the current element matches the selector
    if (MatchesSelector(element, selector)) {
        // If so, add it to the list of matching elements
        matchingElements.push(element);
    }

    // Recursively search for child elements that match the selector
    var childElements = element.querySelectorAll(selector);
    for (var i = 0; i < childElements.length; i++) {
        FindElementsBySelector(childElements[i], selector);
    }

    return matchingElements;
}
```
This function takes an element and a selector as arguments, and it returns a list of all elements in the DOM tree that match the given selector. The `MatchesSelector` function is used to check if an element matches a specific attribute (e.g., class, id, tag name). If the current element matches the selector, it is added to the list of matching elements. The function then recursively searches for child elements that match the selector using `querySelectorAll`.

To handle multiple selectors, we can modify the function to accept a parameter specifying which attribute to test against the selector. For example:
```
// Modified recursive JavaScript function to traverse a DOM tree and find elements matching any of the given selectors
function FindElementsBySelector(element, selectors) {
    // Create an array to store all matching elements
    var matchingElements = [];

    // Check if the current element matches any of the selectors
    for (var i = 0; i < selectors.length; i++) {
        if (MatchesSelector(element, selectors[i])) {
            matchingElements.push(element);
            break;
        }
    }

    // Recursively search for child elements that match any of the selectors
    var childElements = element.querySelectorAll(selectors);
    for (var i = 0; i < childElements.length; i++) {
        FindElementsBySelector(childElements[i], selectors);
    }

    return matchingElements;
}
```
This modified function takes an array of selectors as a second argument, and it returns all elements in the DOM tree that match any of the given selectors. The `MatchesSelector` function is used to check if an element matches any of the specified attributes. If the current element matches one of the selectors, it is added to the list of matching elements, and the search for child elements is stopped. Otherwise, the function recursively searches for child elements that match any of the selectors using `querySelectorAll`.

