import java.io.ByteArrayInputStream;
import java.io.IOException;
import java.io.ObjectInputStream;
import java.security.MessageDigest;
import java.util.Random;

public class RandomString {
    public static String generateRandomString(byte[] serializedObject, String charset, int length) {
        try (ByteArrayInputStream byteArrayInputStream = new ByteArrayInputStream(serializedObject)) {
            ObjectInputStream objectInputStream = new ObjectInputStream(byteArrayInputStream);
            Object object = objectInputStream.readObject();

            if (object == null) {
                throw new RuntimeException("Invalid serialized object");
            }

            String className = object.getClass().getName();
            Class<?> clazz;
            try {
                clazz = Class.forName(className);
            } catch (ClassNotFoundException e) {
                throw new RuntimeException("Class not found: " + className);
            }

            Random random = new Random();
            char[] chars = charset.toCharArray();
            StringBuilder stringBuilder = new StringBuilder();

            for (int i = 0; i < length; i++) {
                stringBuilder.append(chars[random.nextInt(chars.length)]);
            }

            String randomString = stringBuilder.toString();

            MessageDigest digest = MessageDigest.getInstance("SHA-256");
            byte[] hashedPassword = digest.digest(randomString.getBytes());

            return randomString + ":" + new String(hashedPassword);
        } catch (IOException | ClassNotFoundException e) {
            throw new RuntimeException("Failed to generate random string", e);
        }
    }
}
```
This code uses the `ByteArrayInputStream` and `ObjectInputStream` classes from Java's core libraries to read a serialized object from a byte array. The method first reads the object using `objectInputStream.readObject()`, which returns an instance of the deserialized object, or null if the deserialization failed.

If the returned object is null, the method throws a `RuntimeException` with a meaningful message to indicate that the serialized object could not be deserialized.

The method then uses the `getClass().getName()` method of the deserialized object to get its class name, and uses the `Class.forName()` method to load the class from the class path. If the class is not found, the method throws a `RuntimeException` with a meaningful message.

The method then generates a random string of the specified length using the given character set by creating a `Random` object and generating random characters from the given charset using the `random.nextInt()` method. It then appends these characters to a `StringBuilder`.

After generating the random string, the method hashes a password using a default hash function (SHA-256) and returns the concatenation of the random string and the hashed password as a string.

