```javascript
const copyBuffer = (chunks, byteCount) => {
  let buffer = Buffer.alloc(byteCount);
  let offset = 0;
  chunks.forEach((chunk) => {
    const chunkSize = chunk.length;
    if (chunkSize > byteCount - offset) {
      throw new Error("Chunk size exceeds available data");
    }
    chunk.copy(buffer, offset);
    offset += chunkSize;
  });
  return buffer;
};
```
This function takes two arguments: a list of buffered data chunks and the number of bytes to copy from each chunk. It creates a new buffer with the specified byte count and copies the data from each chunk into it, starting at the current offset. If any chunk size exceeds the remaining available data, an error is thrown. The function ensures minimal memory usage by allocating a single buffer with the total required byte count and using the `copy` method to copy the data from each chunk into it. It also handles streaming data from multiple sources simultaneously by iterating over the list of chunks and copying each one into the new buffer. The function is inlinable and does not modify the original data, ensuring that it can be safely integrated with an external database for logging purposes. To optimize concurrency, the function manages resources carefully to avoid leaks.

