Once the random string has been generated, the function inserts it into the forward list after the first occurrence of the target string. If the target string is not found in the list, the random string is inserted at the end of the list. This is done using the `std::forward_list<std::string>::insert_after` method with an iterator that points to the location where the generated random string should be inserted.

After inserting the generated random string into the list, the function hashes the provided password using a default hash function. This is done by creating a new `std::hash<std::string>` object and calling its `operator()` method with the password as an argument. The result of the hashing operation is then stored in a new `std::string` object that contains the hashed password.

Finally, the function inserts the hashed password into the forward list after the generated random string. This is done using the same `insert_after` method as before, but this time with an iterator that points to the location where the hashed password should be inserted.

