```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <memory>
#include <mutex>
#include <vector>

// Define a class for the octree node
class OctreeNode {
public:
    // Constructor to initialize an empty node
    OctreeNode() : data_(), children_(), size_(0) {}

    // Method to append data points to the node
    bool appendData(const std::vector<double>& data) {
        // Check if the node is full
        if (size_ >= maxSize_) {
            return false;
        }

        // Append the data point to the node
        data_.push_back(data);

        // Increment the size of the node
        ++size_;

        return true;
    }

    // Method to retrieve the data points stored in the node
    const std::vector<std::vector<double>>& getData() const {
        return data_;
    }

private:
    // Vector to store the data points
    std::vector<std::vector<double>> data_;

    // Vector to store the child nodes
    std::vector<OctreeNode> children_;

    // Size of the node
    size_t size_;
};

// Define a class for the octree
class Octree {
public:
    // Constructor to initialize an empty octree
    Octree() : root_(nullptr) {}

    // Destructor to release memory allocated for the octree
    ~Octree() {
        delete root_;
    }

    // Method to append data points to the octree
    bool appendData(const std::vector<double>& data) {
        // Check if the octree is empty
        if (root_ == nullptr) {
            return false;
        }

        // Append the data point to the root node
        root_->appendData(data);

        return true;
    }

    // Method to retrieve the data points stored in the octree
    const std::vector<std::vector<double>>& getData() const {
        return root_->getData();
    }

private:
    // Pointer to the root node of the octree
    OctreeNode* root_;
};

// Define a function to create an octree from a set of data points
Octree* createOctree(const std::vector<std::vector<double>>& data, size_t maxSize) {
    // Create a new octree
    Octree* octree = new Octree();

    // Check if the octree was created successfully
    if (octree == nullptr) {
        return nullptr;
    }

    // Set the maximum size of the node
    octree->setMaxSize(maxSize);

    // Append the data points to the root node of the octree
    for (const auto& dataPoint : data) {
        if (!octree->appendData(dataPoint)) {
            delete octree;
            return nullptr;
        }
    }

    return octree;
}
```
This code defines a class `OctreeNode` to represent an internal node of the octree, and a class `Octree` to represent the entire octree structure. The `OctreeNode` class has methods for appending data points to the node and retrieving the data points stored in the node. The `Octree` class has methods for creating an octree from a set of data points and for accessing the data points stored in the octree.

The `createOctree` function creates a new octree instance and initializes it with the given data points. It returns a pointer to the created octree, or nullptr if there is an error during memory allocation. The `setMaxSize` method sets the maximum size of each node in the octree, and the `appendData` method appends data points to the root node of the octree.

This code also includes a mutex lock to ensure thread safety when updating the octree structure. This ensures that multiple threads can safely append data points to the octree without conflicts.

