The `JsonLoader` class has a registry of factory functions, keyed by ID, which can be used to create new instances of subclasses of the base class. The `add_factory_function` method adds a new factory function to the registry with the specified ID, while the `create_instance` method creates a new instance of the subclass using the factory function associated with the given ID.

The `load_files` method loads JSON files from a list of file paths and appends their contents to the `objects` list. If any of the files fail to load or execute, an informative error message is raised. The `execute_files` method executes each object in the `objects` list using the `exec` function.

The `insert_element_in_forward_list` method inserts an element into a forward list-like structure within the JSON objects. It takes a forward list of strings, two string arguments, and an optional position index as input, and it inserts the second string argument after the first occurrence of the first string argument in the forward list based on the provided position index. If the first string argument is not found in the list at the specified position index, the second string argument is inserted at the end of the list.

The `read_string_pairs_from_file` method reads a text file and returns a list of tuples containing each line as a pair of strings.

The `JsonSerialization` class provides static methods for deserializing a byte array into an object and generating a random string from it, using a similar approach to Java serialization but within the Python ecosystem. The `deserialize` method takes a byte array as input and returns an object of any type that can be deserialized from JSON, while the `serialize` method takes an object of any type and returns its JSON representation as bytes.

The `PasswordHashing` class provides static methods for hashing passwords using SHA-256. The `hash_password` method takes a password string as input and returns a hashed password string that includes a randomly generated salt.

