To handle potential errors during the API request, this function gracefully manages any discrepancies between the user-provided text and the book database by returning an empty string if no matching attack is found. It also includes error handling for potential issues with the cache or WebSockets, such as network connectivity problems or malformed JSON responses.

The function uses regular expressions to extract relevant attack names from user-generated text, which allows it to be more flexible and adaptable to different user input scenarios. It also integrates with an external attack database accessible via HTTP requests, which makes it easy to update the list of available attacks in real-time.

Overall, this function is designed to be efficient, scalable, and reliable, making it suitable for high-concurrency environments and supporting up to 1000 characters simultaneously attacking without significant performance degradation.

