Once all checks have passed, the function copies bytes from each buffer in the list into the new buffer using the `buffer.copy()` method, starting at the specified offset and copying up to the end of the buffer. It keeps track of the number of bytes copied so far and stops when the requested byte count is reached.

Finally, it returns the newly created buffer with the copied data. The function also includes error handling for cases where chunk sizes do not match expectations or there are inconsistencies in the data types or formats across different sources. It is important to note that this function modifies the original data chunks by copying their contents into a new buffer, but it does not modify the underlying data structures themselves.

To optimize this function for concurrent access and thread safety, we can use a locking mechanism such as a mutex or a semaphore to ensure that only one thread can access the shared resources at a time. This will help prevent race conditions and improve the overall performance of the function. Additionally, we can use a pool of buffers to avoid creating unnecessary garbage collection pressure by reusing buffers instead of constantly allocating new ones.

The function should also be able to handle different character encodings and perform byte-level operations without relying on high-level string manipulation functions. This can be achieved by using the `Buffer` class's methods such as `writeUInt8()`, `readUInt8()`, `slice()` and `copy()` to perform byte-level operations.

