In this code, we have four types of semaphore managers: `local`, `cloud`, `fallback`, and `semaphore`. The `local` semaphore manager is used for creating local semaphores that are not shared with other processes. The `cloud` semaphore manager is used for creating cloud semaphores that are shared between processes on different nodes. The `fallback` semaphore manager is used for creating fallback semaphores that are used when a cloud semaphore cannot be created or accessed. Finally, the `semaphore` type is used for creating local and cloud semaphores that can be used to synchronize access to shared resources between processes.

Each of these managers has its own set of variables and functions that are used to create and manage semaphores. For example, in the `local` semaphore manager, we have a variable called `semaphores` that is an array of structs, where each struct contains information about a single semaphore. We also have a function called `create_semaphore()` that is used to create a new semaphore and add it to the `semaphores` array. Similarly, in the `cloud` semaphore manager, we have a variable called `cloud_semaphores` that is an array of structs, where each struct contains information about a single cloud semaphore. We also have a function called `create_cloud_semaphore()` that is used to create a new cloud semaphore and add it to the `cloud_semaphores` array.

In addition to these managers, we also have a fallback semaphore manager that is used when a cloud semaphore cannot be created or accessed. This manager has its own set of variables and functions that are used to create and manage fallback semaphores. We also have a function called `create_fallback_semaphore()` that is used to create a new fallback semaphore and add it to the `fallback_semaphores` array.

The code also includes several functions for synchronizing access to shared resources between processes, such as `lock()` and `unlock()`, which are used to acquire and release locks on a shared resource. These functions use the semaphore managers to create and manage semaphores that are used to synchronize access to the shared resource.

The code also includes several functions for cleaning up semaphores, such as `cleanup_semaphore()` and `cleanup_fallback_semaphore()`, which are used to remove semaphores from the managers and release any resources that they were holding. These functions use the mutex locks in the managers to ensure that only one process can access the shared data structures at a time.

In this code, we have used several different techniques for synchronizing access to shared resources between processes, including semaphores, mutex locks, and condition variables. We have also used a combination of these techniques to create fallback mechanisms that allow us to continue running even when a cloud semaphore cannot be created or accessed. By using these techniques, we have been able to create a robust and scalable system for synchronizing access to shared resources between processes.

function collectData() {
  const mongoose = require('mongoose');
  const net = require('net');
  const util = require('util');

  // Connect to MongoDB
  mongoose.connect('mongodb://localhost/mydatabase', { useNewUrlParser: true });
  const db = mongoose.connection;
  db.on('error', console.error.bind(console, 'connection error:'));
  db.once('open', function() {
    // Start collecting data from the network stream
    const stream = net.createConnection({ host: 'localhost', port: 80 });
    stream.on('data', (data) => {
      // Save data to MongoDB
      const collection = mongoose.model('MyCollection');
      const doc = new collection(JSON.parse(data));
      doc.save((err) => {
        if (err) {
          console.error(err);
          return;
        }
        console.log(`Saved data to MongoDB`);
      });
    });
  });

  // Pause/resume functionality
  let paused = false;
  function pause() {
    paused = true;
  }
  function resume() {
    paused = false;
  }

  // Handle network disruptions and database connection issues
  stream.on('error', (err) => {
    console.log(`Network error: ${err}`);
    if (!paused) {
      pause();
      setTimeout(() => resume(), 5000);
    }
  });
  db.on('error', (err) => {
    console.log(`Database connection error: ${err}`);
    if (!paused) {
      pause();
      setTimeout(() => resume(), 5000);
    }
  });
}

