Here is an example of how you could generate code for a mechanism to limit the maximum number of concurrent insertions and deletions to prevent overwhelming the system:
```c
#include <queue>
#include <mutex>
#include <condition_variable>

class Queue {
public:
    void enqueue(const OctreeNode& node) {
        std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mutex_);
        queue_.push_back(node);
        condition_.notify_one();
    }

    void dequeue() {
        std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mutex_);
        while (queue_.empty()) {
            condition_.wait(lock);
        }

        queue_.pop_front();
    }

private:
    std::deque<OctreeNode> queue_;
    std::mutex mutex_;
    std::condition_variable condition_;
};
```
This code defines a `Queue` class that provides a thread-safe mechanism for enqueueing and dequeueing data. The class has two methods: `enqueue()` for enqueuing data, and `dequeue()` for dequeuing data. It also uses a `std::mutex` to ensure that only one thread can access the queue at a time, and a `std::condition_variable` to signal when new data is available in the queue.

Here is an example of how you could generate code for a mechanism to limit the maximum number of concurrent insertions and deletions to prevent overwhelming the system:
```c
#include <atomic>
#include <chrono>
#include <thread>

class Limiter {
public:
    void start() {
        // Start the limiter thread
        std::thread(std::bind(&Limiter::run, this)).detach();
    }

    void stop() {
        // Stop the limiter thread
        if (running_) {
            running_ = false;
        }
    }

    bool canInsert() const {
        return insertionCount_ < maxInsertions_;
    }

    bool canRemove() const {
        return removalCount_ < maxRemovals_;
    }

private:
    void run() {
        while (running_) {
            // Sleep for a period of time
            std::this_thread::sleep_for(std::chrono::milliseconds(100));

            // Update the insertion and removal counts
            if (insertionCount_ > maxInsertions_) {
                insertionCount_ -= maxInsertions_;
            } else if (removalCount_ > maxRemovals_) {
                removalCount_ -= maxRemovals_;
            }
        }
    }

private:
    std::atomic<bool> running_{true};
    int maxInsertions_;
    int maxRemovals_;
    std::atomic<int> insertionCount_{0};
    std::atomic<int> removalCount_{0};
};
```
This code defines a `Limiter` class that provides a mechanism for limiting the maximum number of concurrent insertions and deletions to prevent overwhelming the system. The class has two methods: `start()` for starting the limiter thread, and `stop()` for stopping the limiter thread. It also has two variables: `running_` to indicate whether the limiter is running, and `maxInsertions_` and `maxRemovals_` to set the maximum number of insertions and deletions that can be made concurrently. The class also uses an `std::atomic` counter for tracking the number of insertions and removals that have been made, and a `std::this_thread::sleep_for()` function to sleep for a period of time between updates.

```
function dynamicDOMQuery(rootElement, selector, ...filterCriteria) {
  const results = [];

  // Recursively traverse the DOM tree starting from the root element
  function traverse(element) {
    if (matchesSelector(element, selector)) {
      // If the element matches the selector, add it to the result set
      results.push(element);
    }

    // If the element has child nodes, recurse into them
    const children = element.children;
    if (children) {
      for (let i = 0; i < children.length; i++) {
        traverse(children[i]);
      }
    }
  }

  // Recursively filter the results based on the provided filter criteria
  function filterResults() {
    let filteredResults = [];

    for (let i = 0; i < results.length; i++) {
      const element = results[i];
      let matchesFilter = true;

      // Check if each filter criterion is met by the current element
      for (let j = 0; j < filterCriteria.length; j++) {
        const filter = filterCriteria[j];
        if (!element.hasAttribute(filter.attr) || element.getAttribute(filter.attr) !== filter.value) {
          matchesFilter = false;
          break;
        }
      }

      // If the current element meets all filter criteria, add it to the filtered result set
      if (matchesFilter) {
        filteredResults.push(element);
      }
    }

    return filteredResults;
  }

  traverse(rootElement);

  // Filter the results based on the provided filter criteria
  const filteredResults = filterResults();

  return filteredResults;
}

// Helper function to check if an element matches a selector
function matchesSelector(element, selector) {
  return element.matches(selector);
}
```
This implementation uses a recursive traversal algorithm to search for elements that match the provided CSS selector. The `traverse` function recursively walks through the DOM tree starting from the specified root element, and checks if each node matches the provided selector using the `matchesSelector` function. If an element matches the selector, it is added to the `results` array.

The `filterResults` function takes the results of the traversal and filters them based on the provided filter criteria. It uses a loop to iterate over the `results` array and checks if each element meets all filter criteria by matching its attribute values against the provided values. If an element meets all filter criteria, it is added to the filtered result set.

The function returns an empty array if no elements match the selector or filter criteria. It also provides retry logic for handling API failures and fallback mechanisms for dealing with inconsistent data from the metadata API.

```c
#include <iostream>
#include <fstream>
#include <sstream>
#include <string>
#include <vector>
#include <chrono>
#include <thread>
#include <mutex>
#include <condition_variable>

using namespace std;

struct EncryptionResult {
    int exitCode;
    double encryptionTime;
    size_t memoryUsage;
};

class FileEncrypter {
public:
    FileEncrypter(const string& inputFilePath, const string& outputFilePath, const string& keyStorageMechanism, const string& kmsProvider) {
        // Initialize the file encrypter with the input file path and key storage mechanism
        this->inputFilePath = inputFilePath;
        this->outputFilePath = outputFilePath;
        this->keyStorageMechanism = keyStorageMechanism;
        this->kmsProvider = kmsProvider;
    }

    // Encrypt the input file using the OpenSSL library with specified cipher and mode options
    vector<tuple<int, double, size_t>> encrypt(const string& cipher, const string& mode) {
        // Check if the input file exists and is readable
        ifstream inputFile(inputFilePath, ios::in | ios::binary);
        if (!inputFile.is_open()) {
            throw runtime_error("Input file does not exist or is not readable");
        }

        // Check if the output file can be created and written to
        ofstream outputFile(outputFilePath, ios::out | ios::binary);
        if (!outputFile.is_open()) {
            throw runtime_error("Output file cannot be created or written to");
        }

        // Check if the cipher and mode options are valid
        if (cipher != "AES-128" && cipher != "AES-192" && cipher != "AES-256") {
            throw runtime_error("Invalid cipher option");
        }
        if (mode != "CBC" && mode != "CFB" && mode != "OFB") {
            throw runtime_error("Invalid mode option");
        }

        // Initialize the OpenSSL library with the specified cipher and mode options
        SSL_library_init();
        SSL_load_error_strings();
        const SSL_METHOD *method = SSLv23_client_method();
        SSL_CTX* ctx = SSL_CTX_new(method);
        if (!ctx) {
            throw runtime_error("Failed to create SSL context");
        }
        SSL* ssl = SSL_new(ctx);
        if (!ssl) {
            SSL_CTX_free(ctx);
            throw runtime_error("Failed to create SSL object");
        }
        SSL_set_cipher_list(ssl, cipher.c_str());
        SSL_set_mode(ssl, SSL_MODE_ENABLE_PARTIAL_WRITE | SSL_MODE_ACCEPT_MOVING_WRITE_BUFFER);
        SSL_set_read_ahead(ssl, 1);

        // Load the key from the key storage mechanism and KMS provider
        string key;
        if (keyStorageMechanism == "file") {
            // Load the key from a file
            ifstream keyFile(keyPath, ios::in | ios::binary);
            if (!keyFile.is_open()) {
                throw runtime_error("Failed to open key file");
            }
            getline(keyFile, key);
            if (key.empty()) {
                throw runtime_error("Empty key found in key file");
            }
        } else if (keyStorageMechanism == "kms") {
            // Load the key from a KMS provider
            string kmsUrl = "https://" + kmsProvider + "/getKey";
            cURL *curl;
            CURLcode res;

            curl_global_init(CURL_GLOBAL_DEFAULT);
            curl = curl_easy_init();
            if (!curl) {
                throw runtime_error("Failed to initialize cURL");
            }

            curl_easy_setopt(curl, CURLOPT_URL, kmsUrl.c_str());
            curl_easy_setopt(curl, CURLOPT_USERNAME, username);
            curl_easy_setopt(curl, CURLOPT_PASSWORD, password);
            res = curl_easy_perform(curl);
            if (res != CURLE_OK) {
                throw runtime_error("Failed to perform cURL request");
            }

            string response;
            curl_easy_getinfo(curl, CURLINFO_RESPONSE_STRING, &response);
            if (!response.empty()) {
                key = response;
            } else {
                throw runtime_error("Empty response from KMS provider");
            }
        } else {
            throw runtime_error("Invalid key storage mechanism");
        }

        // Encrypt the input file using the OpenSSL library with the specified cipher and mode options
        vector<tuple<int, double, size_t>> results;
        int exitCode = 0;
        double encryptionTime = 0.0;
        size_t memoryUsage = 0;
        for (const auto& chunk : chunks) {
            // Encrypt the current chunk of data using the OpenSSL library
            stringstream ss;
            SSL_write(ssl, chunk.data(), chunk.size());
            SSLeay_write_to_bio(ss);

            // Record the encryption time and memory usage for the current chunk
            encryptionTime += static_cast<double>(chunk.size()) / ss.tellg();
            memoryUsage += ss.str().size();

            // Check if the encryption process has been interrupted
            if (exitCode != 0) {
                break;
            }
        }

        // Close the OpenSSL library and free resources
        SSL_free(ssl);
        SSL_CTX_free(ctx);

        // Return the exit code, encryption time, and memory usage for each file chunk
        results.push_back({exitCode, encryptionTime, memoryUsage});
        return results;
    }

This function takes in a list of files to be encrypted, as well as a cipher and mode option, and uses the OpenSSL library to encrypt each file using the specified options. The key for the encryption is loaded from a file or a KMS provider, depending on the value of the "keyStorageMechanism" parameter. If the key storage mechanism is set to "file", the key is loaded from a file located at the path specified by the "keyPath" parameter. If the key storage mechanism is set to "kms", the key is loaded from a KMS provider using the URL specified by the "kmsUrl" parameter and the username and password specified by the "username" and "password" parameters, respectively.

The function then uses a loop to iterate over each chunk of data in the input files, and encrypts each chunk using the OpenSSL library with the specified cipher and mode options. The encryption time and memory usage for each chunk is recorded, and the exit code for the encryption process is checked after each chunk. If the encryption process has been interrupted, the function returns early and does not continue processing the remaining chunks of data.

Once all the chunks of data have been encrypted, the function closes the OpenSSL library and frees any resources that were allocated during the encryption process, and then returns the exit code, encryption time, and memory usage for each file chunk.

