### Pagination and Memory Management:

To handle large sets of generated random values efficiently without causing excessive memory usage or blocking the main thread, we can implement pagination in C# by returning an `IEnumerable<double>` instead of a collection of all random values at once. This allows us to only load a subset of the values into memory at a time, which reduces the risk of running out of memory for large sets of data.
```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using UnityEngine;

public class AdvancedRandomValueGenerator {
    public static IEnumerable<double> GenerateRandomValues(int count, double min, double max) {
        yield return UnityEngine.Random.Range((float)min, (float)max);
    }
}
```
In JavaScript, we can use a similar approach by using a pagination library such as `paginate-js`. This library allows us to split an array of values into smaller chunks, which reduces the amount of memory used for large sets of data.
```javascript
const { paginate } = require('paginate-js');

// Usage example
const root = document.querySelector('body');
const selector = 'div';
const testFunc = (element) => element.classList.contains('my-class');
const elements = paginate(findElementsBySelector(root, selector, testFunc), { pageSize: 10 });
console.log(elements);
```
### Error Handling and Robustness:

To handle potential exceptions and edge cases in the C# part, we can add exception handling around the `UnityEngine.Random.Range` function to catch any invalid inputs or exceptions that may occur when generating random values. We can also use a logging library such as `serilog-js` to log all errors and important operations for debugging purposes.
```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using UnityEngine;
using Serilog.Logging;

public class AdvancedRandomValueGenerator {
    private static readonly Logger Log = LogManager.GetLogger(typeof(AdvancedRandomValueGenerator));

    public static float GenerateRandomFloat(double min, double max) {
        try {
            return UnityEngine.Random.Range((float)min, (float)max);
        } catch (Exception ex) {
            Log.Error("Failed to generate random value: {0}", ex);
            return 0;
        }
    }

    public static int GenerateRandomInteger(int min, int max) {
        try {
            return UnityEngine.Random.Range(min, max + 1);
        } catch (Exception ex) {
            Log.Error("Failed to generate random value: {0}", ex);
            return 0;
        }
    }

    public static IEnumerable<double> GenerateRandomValues(int count, double min, double max) {
        for (int i = 0; i < count; i++) {
            try {
                yield return UnityEngine.Random.Range((float)min, (float)max);
            } catch (Exception ex) {
                Log.Error("Failed to generate random value: {0}", ex);
                yield break;
            }
        }
    }
}
```
