### 6. Add the views to the router

Now that we have defined the views, we need to add them to the router so that they can be accessed by the user. We will use the `add` method of the `router` object to add each view function to the router. For example:
```python
from flask import Blueprint
from flask_restful import Api

# Create a blueprint for API endpoints
api_bp = Blueprint('api', __name__)

# Create an API object for handling REST requests
api = Api(api_bp, catch_all_404s=True)

# Add views to the router
api.add_resource(LoginView, '/login')
api.add_resource(LogoutView, '/logout')
api.add_resource(RegisterView, '/register')
api.add_resource(VerifyTokenView, '/verify-token')
api.add_resource(MagicLinkView, '/magic-link')
api.add_resource(UserManagementView, '/manage/users')
api.add_resource(ViewUserView, '/manage/users/<user_id>')
api.add_resource(EditUserView, '/manage/users/<user_id>/edit')
api.add_resource(DeleteUserView, '/manage/users/<user_id>/delete')
```
In this example, we add the `LoginView`, `LogoutView`, and `RegisterView` to the router for handling login, logout, and registration requests. We also add the `VerifyTokenView`, `MagicLinkView`, `UserManagementView`, `ViewUserView`, `EditUserView`, and `DeleteUserView` views for handling verification token and user management requests. The `catch_all_404s=True` argument is used to tell Flask-RESTful to return a 404 error if the requested URL does not match any registered routes.

