import java.io.File;
import java.io.IOException;
import java.util.concurrent.BlockingQueue;
import java.util.concurrent.ExecutorService;
import java.util.concurrent.Executors;
import java.util.concurrent.LinkedBlockingQueue;

public class FileUploadHandler {
    private static final int MAX_CONCURRENCY = 10; // maximum number of concurrent uploads allowed

    public void processFileUploads(BlockingQueue<MultipartFile> uploadQueue, StorageHandlerFactory storageHandlerFactory) throws IOException {
        ExecutorService executor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(MAX_CONCURRENCY); // thread pool for concurrency control

        while (!uploadQueue.isEmpty()) {
            MultipartFile file = uploadQueue.poll(); // get the next file from the queue

            if (file == null) {
                break; // exit if there are no more files to process
            }

            File tempFile = new File(file.getOriginalFilename()); // create a temporary file for storing the uploaded data
            file.transferTo(tempFile); // transfer the file content to the temporary file

            executor.submit(() -> { // submit a task to process the file asynchronously
                try {
                    StorageHandler storageHandler = storageHandlerFactory.create(); // create a storage handler instance
                    storageHandler.store(tempFile); // store the uploaded file in the external storage system
                    tempFile.delete(); // delete the temporary file after successful upload
                } catch (IOException | StorageException e) {
                    System.err.println("Error uploading file " + file.getOriginalFilename() + ": " + e.getMessage());
                } finally {
                    tempFile.close(); // close the temporary file stream to prevent leaks
                }
            });
        }

        executor.shutdown(); // shut down the thread pool when all uploads have been processed
    }
}
```
This solution uses a `BlockingQueue` to handle multiple files concurrently, and an `ExecutorService` to manage the thread pool for concurrency control. The `processFileUploads()` method creates a temporary file on disk for storing the uploaded data, transfers the file content to this file using `MultipartFile.transferTo()`, and then submits a task to the thread pool for processing the uploaded file in an asynchronous manner. If an exception is thrown during the upload process, it is caught and logged, and the temporary file is deleted to prevent leaks. The method also ensures that the `ExecutorService` is shut down when all uploads have been processed, and uses a configurable maximum concurrency value to control the number of concurrent uploads.

This solution balances efficiency, usability, maintainability, and scalability by using a thread pool for concurrency control, managing resources efficiently, and handling exceptions gracefully. It also allows for the addition of new upload handlers or support for different types of file storage systems without significant changes to the overall design.

