        private async Task SetUserLockoutPoliciesAsync(ApplicationUser user)
        {
            // Dynamic password policies based on user roles
            var passwordPolicy = GetPasswordPolicy(user.Role);
            if (passwordPolicy != null)
            {
                await _userManager.SetPasswordPolicyAsync(user, passwordPolicy);
            }

            // Lockout settings
            var lockoutSettings = GetLockoutSettings(user.Role);
            if (lockoutSettings != null)
            {
                await _userManager.SetLockoutSettingsAsync(user, lockoutSettings);
            }
        }

        private PasswordPolicy GetPasswordPolicy(string role)
        {
            switch (role)
            {
                case "Admin":
                    return new PasswordPolicy
                    {
                        RequiredLength = 10,
                        RequireLowercase = true,
                        RequireNonAlphanumeric = false,
                        RequireUppercase = true,
                        RequireDigit = true
                    };
                case "Manager":
                    return new PasswordPolicy
                    {
                        RequiredLength = 8,
                        RequireLowercase = true,
                        RequireNonAlphanumeric = false,
                        RequireUppercase = true,
                        RequireDigit = true
                    };
                case "User":
                    return new PasswordPolicy
                    {
                        RequiredLength = 6,
                        RequireLowercase = false,
                        RequireNonAlphanumeric = false,
                        RequireUppercase = false,
                        RequireDigit = false
                    };
                default:
                    return null;
            }
        }

        private LockoutSettings GetLockoutSettings(string role)
        {
            switch (role)
            {
                case "Admin":
                    return new LockoutSettings
                    {
                        AllowedForNewUsers = true,
                        DefaultLockoutTimeSpan = TimeSpan.FromMinutes(30),
                        MaxFailedAccessAttempts = 5
                    };
                case "Manager":
                    return new LockoutSettings
                    {
                        AllowedForNewUsers = false,
                        DefaultLockoutTimeSpan = TimeSpan.Zero,
                        MaxFailedAccessAttempts = 3
                    };
                case "User":
                    return new LockoutSettings
                    {
                        AllowedForNewUsers = true,
                        DefaultLockoutTimeSpan = TimeSpan.FromMinutes(15),
                        MaxFailedAccessAttempts = 2
                    };
                default:
                    return null;
            }
        }
    }
}
```

This code defines a class called `CustomSignInManager` that inherits from the `SignInManager` class in ASP.NET Core Identity. This class provides an implementation for the `CheckPasswordAsync`, `CheckMfaSignInAsync`, and `SetLockoutSettingsAsync` methods, which are responsible for validating user passwords, checking MFA sign-in attempts, and setting lockout settings for a given user.

The `CustomSignInManager` class also defines a method called `GetPasswordPolicy` that returns a `PasswordPolicy` object based on the provided role string. This method checks the provided role string against a list of predefined roles and returns a password policy based on the corresponding role. If no matching role is found, it returns null.

The `CustomSignInManager` class also defines a method called `GetLockoutSettings` that returns a `LockoutSettings` object based on the provided role string. This method checks the provided role string against a list of predefined roles and returns lockout settings based on the corresponding role. If no matching role is found, it returns null.

### Configure services

Next, you need to configure your ASP.NET Core application to use your custom `SignInManager` implementation. You can do this by adding the following code to your `Startup.ConfigureServices` method:
```csharp
public void ConfigureServices(IServiceCollection services)
{
    // ...

    services.AddIdentity<ApplicationUser, ApplicationRole>()
        .AddSignInManager<CustomSignInManager>();
}
```
This code adds your custom `SignInManager` implementation to the ASP.NET Core application's service collection. When you do this, ASP.NET Core Identity will use your custom implementation instead of the default one.

### Configure controllers and views

Next, you need to configure your ASP.NET Core application's controllers and views to work with your custom `SignInManager` implementation. You can do this by adding the following code to your `Configure` method in your `Startup` class:
```csharp
public void Configure(IApplicationBuilder app, IWebHostEnvironment env)
{
    // ...

    app.UseAuthentication();
}
```
This code configures ASP.NET Core Identity to use authentication middleware in your application. When you do this, ASP.NET Core Identity will start using your custom `SignInManager` implementation instead of the default one.

### Testing with Postman

Now that you have configured your ASP.NET Core application to work with your custom `SignInManager` implementation, it's time to test it out. You can use a tool like Postman to send requests to your API and verify that your custom implementation is working as expected.

To test the password policy check, you can send a POST request to the `/api/Account/Login` endpoint with a JSON payload containing an email address and a password. The password must meet the requirements defined in your `PasswordPolicy`. If it does not, the API will return an HTTP 401 error code indicating that the password was rejected.

To test the MFA sign-in check, you can send a POST request to the `/api/Account/Login` endpoint with a JSON payload containing an email address and a password. The API will then prompt the user for an MFA challenge. If the user provides the correct answer, the API will return an HTTP 200 response indicating that the sign-in was successful. If the user provides an incorrect answer or does not provide an answer at all, the API will return an HTTP 401 error code indicating that the sign-in was rejected.

To test the lockout settings check, you can send a POST request to the `/api/Account/Login` endpoint with a JSON payload containing an email address and a password. If the user enters the correct password too many times in a row (based on the `MaxFailedAccessAttempts` setting), the API will return an HTTP 401 error code indicating that the sign-in was rejected due to lockout settings.

### Conclusion

In this tutorial, you learned how to create and use a custom `SignInManager` implementation in ASP.NET Core Identity. You saw how to configure your ASP.NET Core application to work with your custom implementation and test it out using tools like Postman. By using a custom `SignInManager`, you can add additional functionality to your ASP.NET Core application, such as checking passwords against a password policy or prompting users for MFA challenges before allowing sign-in attempts.

