using System;
using System.Threading.Tasks;
using System.IO;
using System.Text;
using System.Runtime.Serialization.Formatters.Binary;
using System.Net.Sockets;
using System.Net.NetworkInformation;

public class DataLoader {
    public static async Task<T> LoadDataAsync<T>(string filePath) where T : class {
        // Check if the file exists and is a valid binary file
        if (!File.Exists(filePath) || !IsBinaryFile(filePath)) {
            throw new FileNotFoundException($"The file '{filePath}' does not exist or is not a valid binary file.");
        }

        // Create a task to read the data from the file
        Task<T> task = Task.Run(() => {
            using (FileStream fs = new FileStream(filePath, FileMode.Open)) {
                BinaryFormatter formatter = new BinaryFormatter();
                return (T)formatter.Deserialize(fs);
            }
        });

        // Return the task to handle asynchronous operations
        return await task;
    }

    private static bool IsBinaryFile(string filePath) {
        using (FileStream fs = new FileStream(filePath, FileMode.Open)) {
            BinaryReader reader = new BinaryReader(fs);
            byte[] headerBytes = reader.ReadBytes(16);
            return headerBytes[0] == 0x2A && headerBytes[1] == 0x2B; // Check if the file starts with '*' and '+'
        }
    }
}
```
This is a simple C# function that can be used to load data from a binary file asynchronously. The function takes in the path of the file to read from, and returns a task that will complete when the data has been read and deserialized. The function checks if the file exists and is a valid binary file before attempting to read it. If the file does not exist or is not a binary file, an exception is thrown.

To use this function, you can call it as follows:
```
string filePath = "path/to/file.dat";
DataLoader.LoadDataAsync<YourType>(filePath).ContinueWith(task => {
    if (task.IsFaulted) {
        Console.WriteLine("Error loading data");
    } else {
        YourType yourObject = task.Result;
        // Use the deserialized object here
    }
});
```
This will create a new thread to read the file asynchronously and return a task that can be used to handle the asynchronous operation. The `ContinueWith` method is used to execute code when the task completes, either successfully or with an error. In this example, we check if the task has faulted (i.e., an exception was thrown during the async operation) and print an error message if it did. Otherwise, we assign the result of the task to a variable called `yourObject` and use it as needed.

Note that this is just one possible implementation of a data loader function in C#, and there are many other ways to handle asynchronous file I/O and deserialization in .NET. This function is designed to be simple and easy to use, but you may want to consider using more advanced techniques for performance or security if necessary.

