public interface ChunkProcessor {
    void process(byte[] chunk);
}
```
The `FileChunkProcessor` class should contain a method that takes an instance of the `ChunkProcessor` interface as a parameter, allowing users to define their own processing logic. The method should be called for each file chunk, and it should call the `process()` method on the `ChunkProcessor` instance provided by the user.
```java
public class FileChunkProcessor {
    private final ChunkProcessor processor;

    public FileChunkProcessor(ChunkProcessor processor) {
        this.processor = processor;
    }

    public void processFile(String filePath, int chunkSize, int bufferSize) throws IOException {
        try (RandomAccessFile file = new RandomAccessFile(filePath, "r")) {
            long fileSize = file.length();
            byte[] chunk = new byte[chunkSize];
            byte[] buffer = new byte[bufferSize];

            for (long offset = 0; offset < fileSize; offset += chunkSize) {
                int bytesRead = file.read(chunk, 0, chunkSize);
                if (bytesRead != -1) {
                    processor.process(chunk);
                } else {
                    break;
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
```
In this example, the `FileChunkProcessor` class takes a `ChunkProcessor` instance as an argument in its constructor and uses it to process each file chunk. The `processFile()` method reads the file in chunks of size `chunkSize`, using a buffer of size `bufferSize`. If the read operation returns -1, the end of the file has been reached and the loop is exited.
