    float x, y, z; // coordinates of the node center
    std::vector<int> children; // ids of child nodes
    std::set<int> dataPoints; // ids of data points stored in this node
};

class Octree {
public:
    Octree(float x_min, float x_max, float y_min, float y_max, float z_min, float z_max);
    ~Octree();

    int insertDataPoint(int id, float x, float y, float z);
    void deleteDataPoint(int id);
    bool query(float x, float y, float z, std::vector<int>& result);

private:
    int allocateNode(float x, float y, float z);
    void freeNode(int nodeId);
    void rebalance();
    void updateBoundingVolumeHierarchy();
    bool isValidDataPoint(int id);

    std::vector<OctreeNode> nodes; // vector of all nodes in the octree
    std::map<int, int> dataPointsMap; // maps data point ids to node ids
    float x_min, x_max, y_min, y_max, z_min, z_max; // bounding box of the octree
    int nextNodeId; // next available id for a new node
    int maxConcurrentInsertions; // maximum number of concurrent insertions
    int maxConcurrentDeletions; // maximum number of concurrent deletions
    std::mutex mutex; // mutex to protect the octree and dataPointsMap from concurrent access
    std::condition_variable insertionCV, deletionCV; // condition variables for insertion and deletion operations
};
```
This is a C++ implementation of an octree data structure for spatial partitioning in a 3D simulation environment. The `Octree` class has the following methods:

* `Octree(float x_min, float x_max, float y_min, float y_max, float z_min, float z_max)`: Constructs an octree with the given bounding box dimensions.
* `~Octree()`: Destroys the octree and frees all allocated memory.
* `insertDataPoint(int id, float x, float y, float z)`: Inserts a data point into the octree at the given coordinates. Returns the node ID of the newly inserted data point.
* `deleteDataPoint(int id)`: Deletes a data point from the octree by its ID.
* `query(float x, float y, float z, std::vector<int>& result)`: Performs a spatial query on the octree for all data points within a given bounding box centered at (x, y, z). The results are stored in the provided vector `result`. Returns true if the query was successful, false otherwise.
* `allocateNode(float x, float y, float z)`: Allocates a new node in the octree at the given coordinates and returns its ID.
* `freeNode(int nodeId)`: Frees the memory allocated to the specified node.
* `rebalance()`: Rebalances the octree by splitting nodes that are too large and merging nodes that are too small.
* `updateBoundingVolumeHierarchy()`: Updates the bounding volume hierarchy for the octree, which is used for spatial queries.
* `isValidDataPoint(int id)`: Checks if a given data point ID is valid in the octree.

The class also has several private variables and methods to manage concurrent access to the octree and dataPointsMap, limit the maximum number of concurrent insertions and deletions, and handle resource management and dynamic rebalancing.

