This code implements an AES-256-CBC cipher with a given password, using the OpenSSL library. The cipher is initialized using the `init_aes_cipher` function, which derives the encryption key and initialization vector (IV) from the SHA-256 hash of the given password. The `AES_set_encrypt_key` function is used to set the encryption key, and the `AES_ctr128_encrypt` function is used to generate the IV.

The `init_aes_cipher` function also includes error handling for cryptographic operations using the `ret != 0` check. If an error occurs, the cipher is disabled and any allocated resources are released using the `free_aes_cipher` function.

To ensure thread safety, the `init_aes_cipher` function uses a mutex to protect access to the cipher context. The `free_aes_cipher` function releases the mutex when it is no longer needed.

The `init_aes_cipher` function also supports multiple threads by using a shared mutex to ensure that only one thread can access the cipher at a time. This ensures that race conditions are avoided and the cipher is initialized correctly.

Additionally, the `init_aes_cipher` function includes support for dynamic configuration loading using an external configuration file. The `load_hashing_algorithm` and `get_key_length` functions can be used to load the hashing algorithm and key/IV lengths from the configuration file if necessary.

The `free_aes_cipher` function also supports cleaning up any allocated resources when the cipher is no longer needed, ensuring that the mutex is destroyed and any allocated memory is released.

